---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";

const CATALOG = "server/src/iki_server/runs/catalog.py";
const COMPUTE = "spec/src/iki_spec/compute.py";

const H = {
  short: "How a deployment supplies an image Iki can't ship",
  how: "The environment asks; the deployment binds",
  example: "A lab's licensed single-cell tool, bound and run",
  rules: "Matching, pinning, leasing and reuse",
  ref: "Code and decisions",
};
const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: H.short },
  { id: "how", label: H.how },
  { id: "example", label: H.example },
  { id: "rules", label: H.rules },
  { id: "ref", label: H.ref },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title={H.short}>
    <p>
      Some licensed tools may only run from an image the customer supplies. A plugin marks such an
      environment <code>customer_image_required: true</code>, for its container or VM actions. The
      deployment's <code>compute.yaml</code> adds an <code>EnvironmentImageBinding</code>: the
      environment, a range of the plugin's releases, an image pinned by digest, and an optional pull
      Secret. Exactly one binding may match the installed release, or the action is unavailable and
      its launch refusal says which environment needs one. A run pins the bound image when it's
      queued (<Adr n="0209" />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title={H.how}>
    <p>
      A provisioned environment needs <code>image:</code> pinned by digest, or the flag; the flag on
      an environment with no container or VM action is an error (<Path
        p="spec/src/iki_spec/actions.py"
        sym="customer_image_required"
      />). A binding is one document in <code>compute.yaml</code>, in this shape:
    </p>
    <Excerpt file="spec/src/iki_spec/schemas/environment-image-binding/v1.json" />
    <p>
      The range uses the same comparisons as a plugin's <code>iki</code> constraint, and the
      installed version must match exactly one binding (<Path p={COMPUTE} sym="def image_binding(" />).
      Two bindings that both match the installed release, and a pull Secret on a provisioned
      template that isn't Kubernetes, fail the deployment build. The build lists each bound image among those runs use, so the deployment's
      CI approves it beside the others (<Path p="cli/src/iki_cli/deploy.py" sym="def run_images(" />,{" "}
      <PageLink to="Releases and images" />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={3} title={H.example}>
    <p>
      A lab runs its own single-cell plugin, whose analysis needs a vendor's licensed image that Iki
      can't ship. Its environment declares <code>customer_image_required: true</code>, and the lab's
      Compute admin binds it in the shape <Adr n="0209" /> gives:
    </p>
    <figure class="excerpt">
      <figcaption><Path p="docs/adr/0209-deployments-supply-licensed-oci-images.md" sym="kind: EnvironmentImageBinding" /> · ADR 0209's example</figcaption>
      <pre><code>{`apiVersion: iki/v1
kind: EnvironmentImageBinding
environment: acme/analysis.licensed-tools
plugin_version: ">=1.2,<2"
image: registry.example.org/lab/tools@sha256:aaaa…
pull_secret: lab-registry`}</code></pre>
    </figure>
    <p>
      The pull request's build lists that image with the others runs use; on merge, CI signs it into
      the deployment's approvals, which the chart's image policy trusts. A run of the licensed action
      pins the bound image when it's queued, so retries keep it, and the provisioner's pod carries
      the pull Secret (<Path p="server/src/iki_server/runs/kubernetes.py" sym='pod["imagePullSecrets"]' />).
      On Google Cloud the registry path must also be allowed by Binary Authorization, or the pod is
      refused there (<PageLink to="Cloud infrastructure" hash="rules" />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title={H.rules}>
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="An action with no binding is unavailable" open>
        <p>
          A container or VM action whose environment needs an image, with no binding that matches
          the installed release, is unavailable; the refusal names the environment and the plugin
          version, and says a Compute admin can add a binding (<Path p={CATALOG} sym="def customer_image(" />,{" "}
          <Path p={CATALOG} sym="def customer_image_for(" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="The image is pinned, leased and reused as itself">
        <p>
          The run stores the bound image and its pull Secret's name when it's queued, never the
          Secret's contents (<Path p="server/src/iki_server/runs/models.py" sym="environment_image = " />).
          Only a worker launched for that image and pull Secret may lease it, and a worker that
          registered its own environment under the same name never does (<Path
            p="server/src/iki_server/runs/queue.py"
          />). The image's digest is part of the reuse key, and an exact reproduction needs the same
          image still bound. <PageLink to="Pinning and provenance" /> covers what else a run pins.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Compute admins see each one">
        <p>
          Under Compute → Images, Compute admins see each environment that needs an image, the
          binding its installed release matches, and bindings that match nothing, so stale pins show.
          The page only reads: bindings change only in <code>compute.yaml</code>. Pull Secrets are
          named; their values stay in the cluster (<Path p="web/app/src/pages/compute-images.tsx" />,{" "}
          <Path p="server/src/iki_server/api/compute.py" sym="def list_image_bindings(" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Who uses it today">
        <p>
          No first-party plugin or deployment config declares <code>customer_image_required</code>{" "}
          yet; the code paths are exercised by tests. Iki ships no images of licensed tools: labs
          bring their own licences.
        </p>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title={H.ref}>
    <div class="refs">
      <h3>Where</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Path p={COMPUTE} sym="class ImageBinding" />: the binding, and how one is chosen</li>
        <li><Path p={CATALOG} />: availability, and the image an action gets</li>
        <li><Path p="docs/adr/0209-deployments-supply-licensed-oci-images.md" />: the decision</li>
      </ul>
      <p><PageLink to="Compute tiers" /> covers the tiers these images run on.</p>
      <h3>Decisions</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Adr n="0063" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0209" title /></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
